✅ Theory for Uber Fare Prediction Practical
1. Pre-processing the Dataset

Pre-processing means preparing raw data so that it can be used effectively by machine learning models. The Uber fare dataset contains information like pickup/drop-off coordinates, passenger count, and fare amount.

Key Pre-processing Steps

Handling Missing Values:
Remove or impute rows with missing or null entries (e.g., missing fares or coordinates).

Removing Duplicates:
Ensures the model is trained on unique observations.

Data Type Conversion:
Convert fields like pickup_datetime to datetime format so you can extract features like hour, day, month.

Feature Engineering:

Extract time-based features: hour of day, weekday, month, year.

Compute distance between pickup and drop-off using haversine formula (important predictor of fare).

Handling Invalid Entries:
Remove rows where:

Passenger count ≤ 0 or unrealistic (e.g., > 8)

Coordinates lie outside valid ranges

Fare amount is negative

Scaling or Normalization (if needed):
Linear regression often benefits from feature scaling.

✅ 2. Identifying Outliers

Outliers are abnormal values that distort the model.

How to Detect Outliers

Boxplot / IQR Method:
For variables like fare_amount or distance, values below Q1 − 1.5×IQR or above Q3 + 1.5×IQR are outliers.

Z-Score:
Observations more than ±3 standard deviations from the mean.

Visual Methods:

Scatter plots (fare vs distance)

Distribution plots

Common Outliers in Uber Dataset

Extremely high fares (e.g., > $200)

Very long distances (pickup and drop-off far apart)

Zero distance but positive fare

Negative fares

Outliers can be removed or capped depending on the model.

✅ 3. Checking the Correlation

Correlation shows how strongly variables relate to each other.

Why Correlation Matters

Helps identify features that influence fare amount.

Detects multicollinearity (important for linear regression).

Typical Correlations in This Dataset

Distance is highly positively correlated with fare (main predictor).

Time-based features may show some correlation:

Higher fares during peak hours or weekends.

Passenger count has weak or moderate correlation.

Tools Used

Correlation matrix

Heatmap

Pair plots

✅ 4. Implementing Regression Models
A. Linear Regression

A simple model that assumes a linear relationship between features (distance, time, etc.) and the target (fare).

Sensitive to outliers and multicollinearity.

Good baseline model for continuous prediction tasks.

B. Random Forest Regression

An ensemble model built from many decision trees.

Captures non-linear relationships.

More robust to outliers and noisy data.

Usually performs better for complex datasets like Uber fares.

✅ 5. Model Evaluation and Comparison
Metrics Used

R² Score (Coefficient of Determination):

Measures how well the model explains variance in fare.

Closer to 1 → better performance.

RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error):

Measures average prediction error.

Lower RMSE → better model.

MAE (Mean Absolute Error) (sometimes used):

Average absolute difference between predicted and actual fares.

Typical Results (Expected Trends)

Linear Regression:

Moderate performance

Higher RMSE due to assumption of linearity

Random Forest Regressor:

Higher R²

Lower RMSE

Learns complex patterns (e.g., non-linear relation between distance and fare)

Conclusion

Random Forest usually outperforms Linear Regression for this dataset.

✅ Short Viva Questions & Answers (Useful for practical exam)
Q1. Why calculate distance?

Because fare is strongly dependent on the distance traveled; it's the most important predictor.

Q2. Why use Random Forest?

It handles non-linear relationships and outliers better than linear regression.

Q3. What does R² represent?

It shows how much of the variation in fare is explained by the model.

Q4. Why remove outliers?

Outliers distort the regression line and reduce prediction accuracy.

Q5. Why extract datetime features?

Fare pricing may vary by hour, day, or month, so these features improve predictions.
